oard of Occupational Therapy Practice, Board of Social Worker Licensure, Board of Osteopathic Medicine, and the Board of Licensure in Medicine to obtain national and state fingerprint-based criminal history record information for certain applicants for initial licensure and licensure\nby endorsement by each board, licensees seeking a compact privilege who have designated this State as the app|icant`s home state, and individuals seeking a multistate license pursuant to an interstate compact.\n\nLRO633, item 1 - 132nd Maine Legislature, page 10", "192919": "I\nEire\n;_\nA\n\nSTATE or MAINE\nDEPARTMENT or PROFESSIONAL & FINANCIAL REGULATION\nBOARD or LICENSURE IN MEDICINE\n\nJanet T. Mills\nGovemor\n\nMaroulla S. Gleaton, MD\nChair\n\nTl1\"�ihY Temlnova\nExecutive Director\n\nJoan F` C _�hen\n,\nC�m1T11551�\"e1'\n\nTESTIMONY OF TIMOTHY TERRANOVA\n\nEXECUTIVE DIRECTOR\n\nBOARD OF LICENSURE IN MEDICINE\n\nDEPARTMENT OF PROFESSIONAL AND FINANCIAL REGULATION\n\nL.D. 1854\n\nAn Act to Require the Board of Counseling Professionals Licensure, Board of Dental Practice, Board of\nSpeech, Audiology and Hearing and Board of Occupational Therapy Practice to Obtain Fingerprint based Federal Bureau of Investigation Criminal Background Checks for Initial Applicants and Licensees\nSeeking Compact Privileges\nPresented by Senator Mathieson\nBefore the Joint Standing Committee on Health Coverage, Insurance & Financial Services\n\nMay 7, 2025 at 1:00 p.m.\n\nSenator Bailey, Representative Mathieson, and members of the Health Coverage, Insurance and Financial\nServices Committee, I am Tim Terranova, Executive Director of the Maine Board of Licensure in Medicine (\"BOLIM\"). I am here today to testify in favor of the amendment for LD 1854.\nBOLIM licenses and regulates allopathic physicians and physician assistants in Maine. BOLIM is composed of\n11 members: 6 physicians who actively practice medicine; 2 physician assistants who actively render medical services; and 3 public members. BOLIM's mission is to protect the public by ensuring its licensees are ethical,\nprofessional and competent. It fulfills this Inission by licensing, regulating, and educating physician and\nphysician assistants.\n\nThe 131$` legislature passed a bill enacting the PA Compact in Maine. In order to fully participate in the compact, one of the requirements is that all PA applicants pass a criminal background check. Once the law\nbecame effective a request was sent to the FBI, which declined the request, indicating it does not approve Interstate Compacts.\n\nBOLIM encountered this previously when Maine entered the Interstate Medical Licensure Compact (IMLC) for MDs and DOs. The FBI initially declined the state's request and new, specific language was introduced and\npassed in the legislature clarifying the use and confidentiality of the criminal background check information.\nThe language in the amendment is based on the language that was acceptable to the FBI for use with the IMLC.\n\nThis inability to fully participate in compacts is a national phenomenon affecting multiple states and legislation\nhas been introduced in the US House and Senate (co-sponsored by Senator King) that would instruct the FBI to allow criminal background checks for the purposes of interstate compacts. However, we do not know if, or\nwhen, the legislation might pass and this bill is an attempt to ensure full participation as quickly as possible.\nThank you for the opportunity to provide these comments regarding LD 1854. I would be happy to answer\nquestions now or at the work session.\n\nPhone: (207) 287-3601\n\nOffice Location: 16l Capitol Street Augusta, ME 04330\nMailing Address: 137 State House Station, Augusta, Maine 04333\nwww. m a i nc . gov/Ind\n\nTTY: Please Call Maine Relay 7ll\n\nConsumer Assistance: (888) 365-9964\n\nFax: (207) 287-6590"}